Decision Summary

I am not investing in Amazon at the current price.

This is not because I think Amazon is a weak company, that AI demand is fictional, or that the current infrastructure buildout will necessarily fail.

I believe almost the opposite:

AWS is an exceptional business.

Current AI and cloud demand is real.

Amazon possesses one of the strongest combinations of demand data, compute, capital, logistics, robotics and physical distribution in the world.

Amazon may be building the prerequisites for a much larger long-term transformation.

My strongest long-term hypothesis is that Amazon could eventually become a Generalized Deployment Machine: a system capable of repeatedly connecting abundant intelligence, capital and automated production with still-scarce economic demand.

The reason I am not investing is narrower and more demanding:

At approximately USD 3 trillion of market value, Amazon already has to produce an extraordinary financial outcome for the stock to generate extraordinary returns. The normal AWS, advertising and retail-efficiency bull case may justify the company, but it does not obviously provide the return I require. The larger Generalized Deployment Machine thesis could provide the missing upside, but it is currently the least proven part of the analysis.

My conclusion is therefore:

Exceptional company, strong strategic fit, real long-term optionality, but insufficient evidence-adjusted expected return and insufficient margin of safety at the current price.

This document records why I am allocating 0%, what evidence could change that decision, and how I will distinguish a genuine opportunity from a price decline caused by thesis deterioration.

1. The Ordinary Amazon AI Thesis

The ordinary Amazon AI thesis is straightforward:

AI demand increases→ compute demand increases→ AWS usage increases→ AWS revenue and profit increase

AI can also improve other Amazon businesses:

advertising becomes more targeted and effective,

retail forecasting improves,

customer service becomes cheaper,

warehouses become more automated,

delivery routes become more efficient,

software development becomes faster,

and Amazon can operate more output with less human coordination.

This thesis is real. It is also widely understood.

I do not believe that simply recognizing AI demand, AWS growth or warehouse automation constitutes a sufficient investment edge in one of the most heavily analyzed companies in the world.

The possible edge has to exist at a deeper level: either the market is underestimating the duration and profitability of the current infrastructure cycle, or it is underestimating what the interaction between Amazon's assets could eventually make the company capable of doing.

2. The Economic Principle Behind My Larger Thesis

AI does not eliminate scarcity.

AI moves scarcity.

Many economically valuable activities are expensive today because they require scarce human intelligence and coordination:

software development,

engineering,

analysis,

product design,

management,

planning,

procurement,

marketing,

logistics coordination,

customer service,

and capital allocation.

AI can reduce the cost of many of these activities. Robotics may eventually reduce the cost of increasingly broad categories of physical work.

But cheaper production does not create infinite demand.

A company can become ten times more productive without creating ten times more economic value. If it can produce one billion units but customers only want one hundred million, most of the theoretical capacity has little value.

Therefore:

Ability to scale is not the same as ability to create value.

As intelligence and production become more abundant, the scarce layer may increasingly become:

discovering unsatisfied demand,

selecting the correct opportunity,

combining complementary resources,

deploying physical and digital infrastructure,

measuring real outcomes,

and reallocating resources before the opportunity disappears.

I describe this as Balanced Scaling:

Demand Discovery × Resource Efficiency × Deployment Flexibility

The company with the most compute, robots or capital does not automatically win. The stronger system may be the one that continuously connects scarce resources with the best remaining demand.

3. Why Amazon Is an Unusually Strong Candidate

Amazon already owns major components of such a system.

Demand sensing

Marketplace, Retail, Prime and Advertising produce information about:

what customers search for,

what they buy,

which prices they accept,

what they return,

which products are purchased together,

where demand is emerging,

and how much delivery speed matters.

Physical distribution

Amazon has fulfillment centers, inventory systems, transportation, last-mile delivery, seller infrastructure and procurement capabilities.

Compute and AI infrastructure

Amazon owns AWS, Bedrock, Trainium, Graviton and a rapidly expanding network of data centers, servers, networking and power capacity.

Robotics

Amazon has deployed more than one million robots across its operations. Its robotics systems are already connected to large-scale real economic activity rather than being confined to demonstrations.

Capital

Amazon generates enormous operating cash flow and has repeatedly shown a willingness to reinvest at a scale that few companies can match.

The relevant combination is therefore:

Demand× Capital× Compute× AI× Robotics× Physical Distribution

The assets are individually obvious. My hypothesis concerns the economic value of the connections between them.

4. The Generalized Deployment Machine Thesis

My larger prediction is that AI and robotics may reduce not only the cost of producing a specific good, but the cost of economic reallocation itself.

Today, moving into a new market requires large amounts of human organization:

research,

management,

recruiting,

engineering,

software,

product design,

marketing,

procurement,

supply-chain construction,

and new physical operations.

Agentic AI could automate increasing portions of the cognitive layer:

market analysis→ demand discovery→ product design→ engineering→ procurement→ pricing→ marketing→ monitoring→ reallocation

Robotics could automate increasing portions of the physical layer:

production→ fulfillment→ transport→ maintenance→ construction

If both layers become cheaper and more general, Amazon may become increasingly capable of doing the following:

demand signal→ opportunity evaluation→ capital allocation→ infrastructure deployment→ automated execution→ distribution→ outcome measurement→ resource reallocation→ repeat

In that world, Amazon would not be valuable because customers need an infinite number of packages or because one individual business can grow forever.

It would be valuable because it could repeatedly move resources from saturated demand pool A to less saturated demand pool B, then to C, while reusing horizontal assets such as compute, logistics, advertising, identity, payments, forecasting and distribution.

The company's central asset would increasingly become the machine that creates and scales new assets.

That is what I mean by a Generalized Deployment Machine.

5. A More Plausible Two-Layer Version of the Thesis

The strongest version of the thesis does not require Amazon to centrally predict and produce everything the economy needs.

It can operate through two layers.

Layer 1: External deployment platform

Millions of independent actors discover demand and take product risk:

Marketplace sellers test physical products.

AWS customers test software and AI applications.

Advertisers test commercial demand.

Merchants test pricing, inventory and distribution.

Amazon supplies the infrastructure and collects economic value through compute, logistics, advertising, marketplace fees and services.

This makes Amazon closer to a deployment operating system or deployment toll collector. It benefits from decentralized experimentation without having to correctly select every opportunity itself.

Layer 2: Internal deployment machine

Amazon uses the same infrastructure, data, AI, robotics and operational learning to improve its own businesses and launch new ones.

The external layer is already substantially proven.

The internal generalized-reallocation layer remains much more speculative.

This distinction matters because I should not value the speculative layer as though it were already producing cash flow.

6. What Has Already Been Proven

Amazon's Q2 2026 results materially strengthened the ordinary AWS and infrastructure thesis.

AWS reported approximately:

USD 42.2 billion quarterly revenue,

37% year-over-year growth,

USD 16.6 billion operating income,

and an operating margin of approximately 39.4%.

Amazon reported:

USD 161.4 billion trailing-twelve-month operating cash flow,

USD 169.0 billion of trailing-twelve-month purchases of property and equipment, net of proceeds and incentives,

and trailing-twelve-month free cash flow of approximately negative USD 7.6 billion under Amazon's definition.

Amazon also reported approximately USD 496 billion of remaining performance obligations, primarily related to AWS.

These results reduce the probability of the simplest bear case:

Amazon is building enormous amounts of AI infrastructure for demand that does not exist.

The company remained capacity constrained, and management indicated that a majority of available 2027 cloud capacity and some 2028 capacity had already been reserved.

This is strong evidence of current demand and near-term monetization potential.

It is not yet proof of high returns on the entire next capital cycle.

7. Why the USD 496 Billion Backlog Is Strong but Not Conclusive

The USD 496 billion figure must be interpreted carefully.

Amazon describes it as performance obligations primarily related to AWS for contracts with original terms longer than one year. The weighted-average remaining life of these contracts is approximately 6.4 years. Revenue recognition still depends on customer usage and Amazon's performance.

The concentration also matters.

The expanded OpenAI arrangement includes USD 138 billion of commitments over eight years. A very large contract can make aggregate backlog look more diversified and immediately monetizable than it actually is.

Therefore, the backlog supports the demand thesis, but does not prove:

near-term revenue of the same magnitude,

broad customer diversification,

stable future pricing,

high margins on every contract,

or attractive incremental return on invested capital.

The correct inference is:

Demand visibility is strong, but backlog is not equivalent to profit or ROIC.

8. The USD 220 Billion Capital Expenditure Test

Amazon currently expects approximately USD 220 billion of cash capital expenditure in 2026, up from its earlier expectation of approximately USD 200 billion.

Part of the increase reflects higher memory costs. Therefore:

CapEx growth is not identical to capacity growth.

The expenditure creates real assets: land, power infrastructure, buildings, chips, servers, networking, cooling, fulfillment systems and other long-lived infrastructure.

However, accounting asset creation is not the same as economic value creation.

Cash is certain and flexible. A specialized data center or rapidly aging AI chip is valuable only to the extent that future cash flows justify its cost.

The central question is therefore not whether the assets physically exist. It is:

What additional normalized operating profit and free cash flow will the next dollar of invested capital produce?

The relevant metric is incremental ROIC.

Demand alone is insufficient:

Demand ≠ Revenue ≠ Profit ≠ Free Cash Flow ≠ ROIC

Even fully utilized infrastructure can be a poor investment if prices, margins or asset lives are inadequate.

The financial test will emerge with a lag:

2026 capital expenditure→ 2027–2028 capacity comes online→ customer utilization grows→ revenue and operating profit appear→ depreciation and reinvestment become visible→ normalized free cash flow and incremental ROIC can be evaluated

The present buildout is therefore important for two different reasons:

It may produce strong AWS returns.

It creates infrastructure that could later enable the Generalized Deployment Machine thesis.

Successful AWS monetization would not by itself prove the larger thesis. It would mean that Amazon has successfully built part of the physical and computational substrate the larger thesis requires.

That is an important distinction.

9. Uncertainty Is Potential Only When the Price Compensates for It

Uncertainty can create investment opportunity because share prices reflect probability distributions rather than one fixed future.

If the market initially assigns a high probability to poor capital returns and later evidence shifts probability toward high returns, expected value rises before the final cash flows are fully visible.

Amazon demonstrated this mechanism in 2026.

When the company initially announced approximately USD 200 billion of capital expenditure, the stock fell sharply because required investment increased while the return remained unclear.

After Q2, the stock rose sharply even though the capital-expenditure outlook increased to approximately USD 220 billion. The new information was not lower spending. It was stronger evidence that Amazon had real demand and a plausible path to monetization:

AWS growth accelerated,

margins remained exceptional,

backlog increased,

and capacity remained constrained.

This was a direct case of:

Uncertainty resolution→ higher estimated return on capital→ revaluation

However:

Uncertainty is not automatically upside.

It is valuable only when the market assigns a worse probability distribution than the evidence justifies.

At the current price, a meaningful part of the easiest uncertainty has already been resolved positively and reflected in the stock.

The remaining uncertainty concerns a harder question:

Can Amazon repeatedly monetize hundreds of billions of new capital at high returns after competition, falling compute costs, depreciation and technological obsolescence?

