12. Why I Am Not Assigning Value to the Entire Optionality Today

The Generalized Deployment thesis requires several conditions to become economically real:

AI must reduce the cost per economically useful decision, not merely improve benchmark capability.

Agents must reliably move from answers to planning, tool use, execution, monitoring and reallocation.

Robotics must become more general across tasks.

Amazon must connect demand signals to capital allocation better than competitors.

New valuable demand pools must continue to emerge.

Amazon must reallocate capital faster without destroying decision quality.

Horizontal infrastructure must be reusable across new opportunities.

Energy, chips, land, water, grid access and regulation must not become binding constraints that transfer the economic rents elsewhere.

Incremental ROIC must remain attractive at enormous scale.

AI must strengthen Amazon faster than it reduces the cost of building competitors.

Amazon's organizational bureaucracy must not neutralize its technological flexibility.

Several of these conditions may occur.

They have not yet occurred strongly enough to justify treating the full thesis as a base-case cash flow.

Optionality has value, but optionality is not free merely because it cannot yet be measured. At USD 3 trillion, I must ask whether I am receiving the option cheaply or already paying for substantial success.

My current answer is that the price does not clearly offer the option cheaply enough.

13. The Main Counter-Thesis

AI may reduce Amazon's internal coordination costs.

It may also reduce the coordination and entry costs of thousands of smaller competitors.

If external market coordination becomes much cheaper, the winning structure may not be one giant corporation internalizing more economic activity. It may be a modular network of specialized companies that can coordinate through software and agents.

This creates a split outcome:

AWS and Amazon's external infrastructure may still win by supplying those companies.

Amazon's internal conglomerate-like deployment advantage may be weaker than expected.

In other words, AI could strengthen Amazon as infrastructure while weakening the relative advantage of Amazon as a centralized operator.

My thesis must therefore answer:

Does AI reduce Amazon's cost of deployment faster than it reduces everyone else's cost of competing with Amazon?

That question is still open.

14. What Could Go Wrong Even If AI Demand Remains Enormous

The investment can underperform without the AI revolution failing.

Compute volume rises while prices collapse

Revenue depends on volume × price. Profit additionally depends on margin and reinvestment requirements.

Hardware efficiency improves faster than usage grows

If the compute required per task falls dramatically, aggregate demand must grow even faster to absorb capacity.

Hyperscalers collectively overbuild

Amazon, Microsoft, Google, Oracle and others are expanding simultaneously. Industry demand can remain large while industry returns deteriorate.

Customer AI returns disappoint

Customers ultimately need AI to reduce costs, increase revenue or improve productivity. If enterprise returns disappoint, budgets can contract.

Hardware becomes obsolete too quickly

Data-center structures can last for decades; AI accelerators may have much shorter economic lives. High recurring replacement needs can weaken owner earnings.

Large customers dominate demand

Large long-duration commitments create visibility but can also create concentration, bargaining power and renewal risk.

Value flows to bottlenecks rather than Amazon

If power, grid capacity, memory, networking or advanced chips remain scarcer than cloud distribution, more of the economic rent may accrue to suppliers.

Technology works but the stock does not

Amazon can be strategically correct while investors earn mediocre returns because too much success was already embedded in the purchase price.

This last case is central to my present decision.

15. Why I Am Not Waiting for Complete Certainty

My decision is not:

I will wait until the Generalized Deployment Machine is completely proven.

That would be structurally too late. Markets update probabilities before a transformation becomes fully visible.

The likely evidence sequence, if the thesis is correct, is approximately:

2026–2027: prerequisite construction

Massive AI infrastructure, custom chips, more robotics and early agentic systems.

2027–2029: first major capital-return test

The 2026 infrastructure begins generating revenue, operating profit and depreciation. The market can start estimating incremental returns more reliably.

2028–2030: first serious evidence for the larger thesis

Possible signals include output growing faster than human input, more general robotics, improving Retail economics, shorter time to launch new operations and visible reuse of infrastructure.

2030–2035: strong-form test

If the thesis is correct, Amazon should increasingly demonstrate that its reinvestment runway and deployment ability extend beyond the normal maturation of AWS, Retail and Advertising.

Amazon could trade far above today's price before the full thesis is proven because AWS, Ads and Retail provide a financial bridge.

I am therefore not betting on a guaranteed decline.

I am refusing to pay the current price without either a better expected-return asymmetry or materially stronger evidence.

